Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
just tell us what is the government  advice right now as we uh prepare  ourselves for this record-breaking heat  wave  so our advice is is broadly for people  for themselves to be sensible drink lots  of water stay in the shade we've got a  hot 48 hours coming but also be very  aware of those vulnerable groups who we  think are most likely to succumb to the  heat so that's the very young small  infants  but particularly the elderly often  living alone who may struggle in the  heat and who i think would probably  appreciate the neighborly thing being  done which is for people to go and knock  on their door and just check that  they're okay and they have access to  water you know many of us are used to  traveling overseas for this kind of heat  we don't see it and i haven't seen it  before in this country so we sort of  broadly know what to do and what we're  asking people to do is is just take it  easy in particular there are going to be  disruptions on the trout transport  network um and so where the transport  operators have advised people to avoid  traveling unless they have to obviously  if you're using public transport you  need to bear that in in mind but as i  say we've got a hot 48 hours coming and  we're asking people just to be prepared  and look out for the vulnerable  now your colleague dominic rob was on  with sophie ridge yesterday i mean i'm  sure you would have seen it i know  you're a avid watcher of the political  sunday morning shows and he said that we  ought to enjoy the sunshine and be  resilient enough um i mean is that  sensible advice there is exactly what he  said we ought to enjoy the sunshine and  actually we ought to be silly enough  though through some of the pressures it  will place do you think we should be  enjoying the sunshine  well look if you're lucky enough not to  be working today and you're going to the  beach obviously i know you'll take lots  of sun cream and water and a umbrella  and all those kind of things and be  sensible but we need to recognize that  there are some people who will not cope  well in the heat  and what we want is for everybody to  look out for them  lots of people will go about their lives  perfectly normally so for example we  have said that school should stay open  and kids should go to school that are  very often they're safer in schools and  kids need to learn obviously and schools  can look after them and hydrate them and  keep them nice and uncool but you know  we just need to be sensible we haven't  seen this kind of heat before in the  country uh we need to learn from it and  understand how we operate and adapt our  behavior so we all get through it safely  so with ferocious temperatures she's  still happy for people to go to the  beach and hang out all day in the sun  well some people may wish to um  obviously and it's it'll be cooler at  the coast than it is in the um in the  center of the country particularly in  the midlands and in in london but what  we're saying to people is that they need  to take responsibility for themselves  recognize that this is a really  ferocious heat that we haven't seen in  this country before and adapt their  behavior accordingly now that will  change from person to birth  very elderly people need to understand  that it will be difficult for them in  the heat we all need to understand that  and we want to look out for them right  and particularly small infants uh but i  think you know lots of people understand  that we're just trying to alert people  to the fact that this heat is coming for  the next 48 hours and they need to adapt  their behavior accordingly  i mean we've known this forecast has  been in place for a couple of weeks now  um what mitigation has been put in place  to make sure that schools can stay open  that hospital appointments can be kept  that the trains keep running what's the  government been doing in the lead up to  this to make sure we can kind of live  our lives as much as possible  well obviously as it became clear that  the the weather was going to produce  this record heat and there's a strong  possibility that we'll hit the all-time  record in the next 48 hours we have been  in close contact with all the public  services to make sure that they are as  ready as they can be so guidance being  issued for schools for example so they  can keep children safe and hydrated  making sure that the health service is  aware that it's coming and that they've  got capacity stood up we particularly  look for example at the 999 service  making sure that the 2400 extra staff  we've got in there that extra capacity  will be able to cope so we're sort of  getting everybody stood up and ready for  the next 48 hours and we just need to to  get through that learn from it and then  wait for the cooler air to arrive on  wednesday  so for schools that think that they  won't be able to deal with the  temperatures and the heat that the kids  are facing to think they should close  well we've said that that school should  stay open but that it is a decision for  the local head teacher now there are  some  schools where obviously the building or  for other reasons they may not feel that  they're able to keep the kids nice and  nice and safe and cool and hydrated and  if that is so then obviously it's a  decision for the head teacher but for  the vast majority we believe they should  stay open  okay um you shared a cobra meeting over  the weekend does the prime minister  there  uh he wasn't but it is my job to check  over meetings and i briefed him  yesterday morning at about eight o'clock  personally  i mean we've got a red extreme heat  warning do you not think the prime  minister should perhaps have been at  that meeting  no it's literally my job to chair cobra  i in my department i have the civil  contingency secretariat which is  responsible for coordinating the  response across the whole of government  and that's what we've done in the the  two cobra meetings that we've had and as  i say i briefed the prime minister  yesterday morning at eight o'clock  myself  yeah and he where was he this weekend mr  mothers  i don't know his exact whereabouts so  briefed him i can tell you  he was at checkers having a party  all right oh yes sorry i did read that  in the media yes  there he is  so while the country was preparing for  its worst heat wave in a generation the  prime minister was having a party at  checkers  well i have to tell you having chad  cobra on on saturday and then um uh  briefed the prime minister at 8am on  sunday i did have some of my family  around for lunch in my constituency on  sunday lunchtime as well  i know but you're not prime minister sir  i mean do you think that the prime  minister's just kind of decided you know  he's going to be leaving the job pretty  soon anyway so there's no point in him  paying attention and being part of these  very important meetings  so as i say that's completely unfair we  communicated on on the saturday or  friday as well and then as i say i  briefed him at eight o'clock on on  sunday morning so he's right across this  i think this is a very unfair  characterization of the media um around  this particular issue right it is my job  to chair cobra it's my job to to  coordinate across government and then to  be accountable to him which is exactly  what i did yesterday morning when i  briefed him i mean you know the point  i'm making him smarter he goes to  leadership doesn't it and you would have  thought that when we're facing something  like this the leader of the country  would want to be in the meeting so he  can perhaps lead  our response  well the prime minister has to deal with  lots of things on a daily basis that's  why he like organizing parties at  checkers  that's why he appoints secretaries of  state to deal with those those issues  and as i say we are now well coordinated  we're stood up we're facing a difficult  uh 48 hours possibly and that we  hope and expect that people understand  that it's going to be very hot they'll  look out for the vulnerable and adapt  their behavior so we can all get through  it as safely as possible just a final  thought on this mr martin before we move  on to uh talking about uh the new  leadership race um you said that we are  prepared and we've sort of stood  everyone up  in the event that there is another heat  wave coming do you think that we could  end up drafting in um more assistance  perhaps from the military  well i i can't uh comment on that at the  moment but obviously we keep all our  options open for for where things may go  you know we want to make sure that we  learn from the next 48 hours about how  we can adapt if it does occur again but  look the weather is unpredictable let's  hope it doesn't  okay let's talk about the leadership  race you will have seen the debates uh  friday and last night i mean what do you  make of the way that the candidates are  going at each other  well to be honest with you i mean i  think it's good to have a vigorous  debate uh within any kind of uh  political organization you are our  democracy and reaching the right answers  for the future of our country requires  this kind of challenge and battle of  ideas um and so i was one of the other  socialists  well you know i mean i i generally uh  adhere to the reagan doctrine of not  speaking ill of another conservative i  suppose some people might see  being called a socialist an insult i'm  not sure that's true in the labour party  but there we are um but you know it's  it's i guess it's a sign of a kind of  vigorous battle of ideas and we need to  have that battle so that we can select  the right leader with the right  solutions and then unite behind that  person i mean battle of ideas is one  thing but this is a very public tearing  down of one another and it's not just  about ideas isn't it you could see that  there were points last night but he got  very personal especially between richie  sunak and liz trust i mean what's that  going to do to the party when it tries  to come back together once this is all  over  well look i'm confident uh the  conservative party like any family that  has its internal rows and discussions  sometimes in public um uh that  eventually will come back together again  in the spirit of harmony and love and  unite to win the uh general election you  know it's important that people  recognize that all political parties are  kind of standing coalitions and the  conservative party is the same and a  vigorous exchange of ideas in what is a  challenging time for the country should  be expected when you're talking about  such important issues and the leadership  of a g7 nation if it was just a sort of  polite you know agreement and consensus  across the board there wouldn't be much  point in having the competition at all  so with that in mind then who are you  going to be backing mr malthouse  i haven't declared yet there's still a  long way to go that's why i'm asking you  well i'm going to be listening to the  arguments over the next 24 48 hours and  voting accordingly  i mean who do you think did well last  night wish you sunic  well i think they all did well in their  own way i'm sorry to hedge my bets but  um as i say i i thought they all did  well and we'll have to see how the um  the voting goes today you know in my own  mind i'm kind of keeping a  running tally of the various arguments  and as i approach voting this afternoon  i'll be voting according  okay kit well i do appreciate your time  today uh thank you very much  thanks a lot cheerio

